<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conrad I of Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 881–<a href="/years/918">918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Conrad the Younger, Conrad I of East Francia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Conrad I was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> from 911 to 918, the first king not of the Carolingian line. A member of the Conradine family and Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, he was elected after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a> but struggled against the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raids. On his deathbed he recommended Henry of Saxony as his successor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-germany.jpg" alt="Conrad I of Germany" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KonradSiegel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Conrad, called the Younger, was the son of Duke Conrad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, called the Elder, and his wife Glismoda, who was probably related to Ota, wife of the Carolingian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf of Carinthia</a>. The Conradines, counts in the Franconian Lahngau region, had been loyal supporters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>, and they competed with the sons of the Babenberg duke Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> for predominance in Franconia. In 906 the two parties fought near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a>. Conrad the Elder was killed, as were two of the three Babenberg brothers, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a> took the Conradines' side. Conrad then became the undisputed duke of all Franconia. He failed, however, to extend Conradine rule over western Lotharingia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a>, Conrad was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> on 10 November 911 at Forchheim by the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. He was the first king not of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a>, the first elected by the nobility and the first to be anointed. The dukes kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>, from the throne. Only Reginar, duke of Lotharingia, refused allegiance and joined West Francia.</p><p>Because he was himself one of the dukes, Conrad found it hard to establish his authority over them. Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> rebelled against him until 915, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burchard-ii-duke-of-swabia">Burchard II</a> of Swabia demanded and received more autonomy. Erchanger of Swabia revolted in 913, defeated the royal army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> and was executed on 21 January 917 with his brother Berthold. Arnulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> called on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> for help. His campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a> to regain Lotharingia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> failed. The Magyar raids, continuing since the Bavarian defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pressburg">Battle of Pressburg</a> in 907, further weakened his authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Severely injured in one of his battles with Arnulf, Conrad died on 23 December 918 at Weilburg Castle and was buried in Fulda Cathedral. According to the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a>, he persuaded his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-of-franconia">Eberhard of Franconia</a> on his deathbed to offer the crown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and one of his main opponents, whom he thought the only duke able to hold the kingdom together against the rivalries of the dukes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raids.</p><p>Eberhard and the Frankish nobles accepted this advice in May 919, and Henry was elected king at the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a>. Kingship passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. Eberhard succeeded Conrad as duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and was killed in 939 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-andernach">Battle of Andernach</a> during a rebellion against Otto I; the duchy then became a direct possession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a> until 1024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conrad I of Germany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Adalbert of Babenberg was executed in 906, <b>Conrad the Younger</b> became the leading magnate, recognised as duke of the Franks.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-franconia">Duchy of Franconia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 915 Count Erchanger defeated King <b>Conrad I</b>'s forces at Wahlwies and was proclaimed duke; Conrad executed him in 917, but Burchard II established the duchy, recognised by Henry the Fowler in 919.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-swabia">Duchy of Swabia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.48,8.25&amp;year=905" aria-label="Weilburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Conrad I of Germany"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5447 5782h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5447 5782h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.48,8.25&amp;year=905">What was here in 905 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
